ting_output.shape[-1] (host metadata, no sync). Wrapped signature should also handle kwargs. Write like:\n\n```python\ndef wrapped(*a, **kw):\n    res = orig(*a, **kw)\n    try:\n        gating = kw.get(\"gating_output\", a[1] if len(a) > 1 else None)\n        n_local = gating.shape[-1] if gating is not None and gating.dim() >= 1 else None\n        topk_ids = res[1] if isinstance(res, tuple) and len(res) >= 2 else None\n        if topk_ids is not None:\n            _record(topk_ids, n_local)\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    return res\n```\n\n_record(topk_ids, num_local_experts): if num_local_experts is None, fall back to... skip recording? Slot-0 trick needs buffer of size n_local+1. If n_local is None, skip (or derive from ids.max()+1 — sync — no). Skip with counter? Keep it simple: if n_local is None: return. Hmm, but that could silently record nothing. In FusedTopKRouter, gating_output is always the router logits [M, E]. Fine.\n\nKeep the no-sync discipline comments, the two-stage gate, swallowing failures, atexit flush, TSV output `expert_union_<pid>.tsv` (maybe `fn_expert_union_<pid>.tsv`; keep as upstream name? adapt prefix: \"fn_expert_union\" print messages). Print tag \"[fn_expert_union]\".\n\nAlso default off: _ENABLED = FN_EXPERT_UNION == \"1\"; install() returns if not enabled. Two-stage gate note: caller may also check. Keep lazy install rationale: wrap at install() time — explain that install is lazy because the MoE layers are built by the first decode.\n\nAlso upstream's `wrapped._ds4_orig = orig` → `wrapped._fn_orig = orig`.\n\nEnv defaults: FN_EU_OUT default \"~/vllm-prof\" — keep.\n\nOne more consideration: acceptance criterion also runs `python3 -m unittest tests.test_instruments -v` from the repo root. tests package: does tests/ have __init__.py? `python3 -m unittest tests.test_instruments` requires tests to be a package, or works via unittest's namespace? `tests.test_instruments` requires tests to be importable — without __init__.py, does python3 -m unittest tests.test_instruments work? unittest uses importlib; since Python 3.11+, namespace packages work for `tests.test_instruments` if cwd is on sys.path. Actually `python3 -m unittest tests.test_instruments` converts to a module path and imports — namespace packages are fine (does the repo have tests/__init__.py? ls shows only test_repo_invariants.py). And existing harness presumably runs `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`. To be safe, my tests should not depend on being imported as package (use ROOT-relative pathlib). Should I add __init__.py? The boundary says only touch container/rootfs/ and tests/ — adding tests/__init__.py is allowed but changes repo structure; `python3 -m unittest tests.test_instruments` on py3.11+: namespace package import works. Let me verify with python version in sandbox: python3 --version. Then run acceptance command to verify.\n\nEnvironment: does sandbox have python3? Probably. torch not needed.\n\nAlso verify: does this repo's CI run flake/style? Not mentioned. Style reference receipts-verify.py: plain stdlib, docstring header, type hints in some places.\n\nOK let me write the files.\n\nOne more check: \"All paths touched by any task commit … must remain inside conflictDomains\" — my commit adds container/rootfs/fn_*.py and tests/test_instruments.py. Fine. Don't touch container/rootfs/.keep.\n\nLet me write fn_synctrace.py:\n\n```python\n# Adapted from ds4_synctrace.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d\n# (container/rootfs/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ds4_synctrace.py),\n# Apache-2.0 licensed; DS4_ env surface renamed to FN_ and the profile-window\n# driver re-targeted to flashnext. Upstream notice retained below.\n\"\"\"Attribute blocking device->host syncs to python call sites.\n\n...adapted docstring...\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nHmm: \"notice header travels\" — header comment at top is enough.\n\nFor the docstring, adapt:\n\n```\n\"\"\"Attribute blocking device->host syncs to python call sites.\n\nAdapted from ds4_synctrace.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d (Apache-2.0).\nUpstream measured hipMemcpyWithStream at 42% of decode self-CPU and\nhipStreamSynchronize at 16% in its 2026-08-07 profile; ...\n```\n\nWeave the \"Adapted from\" line into both the header comment and the docstring? A single location is sufficient for grep. Put the notice as the first lines of the docstring, and/or as a comment. Upstream offload_batch puts SPDX first, then docstring. I'll do:\n\n```python\n# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n# Adapted from ds4_synctrace.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d,\n# Apache License 2.0. Changes: DS4_ prefix -> FN_, ...\n```\n\nFor consistency, SPDX + Adapted-from header comment in all three files, plus mention in docstring.\n\nWait — one nuance: original upstream files ds4_synctrace.py and ds4_expert_union.py didn't have SPDX lines, only offload_batch did. Adding SPDX to adapted copies is fine.\n\nOK, now let me write the three files.\n\nfn_synctrace.py contents — adapt the body: keep everything, replace docstring DS4 references. Frame key split 'site-packages/' — keep (instrument lands in site-packages inside the image).\n\nfn_expert_union.py — write carefully.\n\n```python\n# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n# Adapted from ds4_expert_union.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d\n# (Apache License 2.0). Adaptations:\n#   - DS4_ env prefix -> FN_ (kill switch FN_EXPERT_UNION, default off)\n#   - the wrap is RE-TARGETED: upstream wrapped\n#     vllm...fused_moe.experts.gpt_oss_triton_kernels_moe.make_routing_data,\n#     a routing entry the flashnext workload never calls\n#     (specs/flashnext/evidence/qsa-53896.md section 4). This wraps the stock\n#     fused-mixture router entry fused_topk in\n#     vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/router/fused_topk_router.py.\n\"\"\"FN_EXPERT_UNION=1: measure how many DISTINCT experts a decode step touches.\n\n...\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nDocstring: adapt upstream's rationale. Qwen4-Flash-Next top_k is? Don't fabricate numbers like \"6-per-token / ~34 experts / K=6\" — those are DS4-specific. Rewrite the rationale generically: the decode roof is dominated by routed-expert bytes, which scale with the union of experts the step's positions select, not with top-k per token; correlated routing shrinks the union; nothing static can settle it — it has to be measured against real traffic. This is faithful and generic.\n\nBody:\n\n```python\nimport atexit\nimport os\n\nimport torch\n\n_ENABLED = os.environ.get(\"FN_EXPERT_UNION\") == \"1\"\n_START = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_EU_START\", \"200\"))\n_CALLS = int(os.environ.get(\"FN_EU_CALLS\", \"2000\"))\n_OUT = os.path.expanduser(os.environ.get(\"FN_EU_OUT\", \"~/vllm-prof\"))\n```\n\n_record(topk_ids, num_local_experts): similar to upstream, but `if num_local_experts is None: return` guard… wait, upstream signature was _record(topk_ids, num_local_experts). Keep it, add guard.\n\nActually note: `seen[ids + 1] = True` requires ids >= -1. ids may be uint? fused_topk returns int32/int64 topk_ids. .long() conversion handles that.\n\nWait, subtle: -1 padding absorption: seen has size n_local+1; ids are in range [-1, n_local-1]; ids+1 → [0, n_local]. OK.\n\ndef install():\n\n```python\ndef install():\n    \"\"\"Idempotently wrap fused_topk. Safe to call more than once.\"\"\"\n    global _installed\n    if not _ENABLED or _installed:\n        return\n    try:\n        from vllm.model_executor.layers.fused_moe.router import (\n            fused_topk_router as m,\n        )\n    except Exception as exc:\n        print(f\"[fn_expert_union] not installed: {exc}\", flush=True)\n        return\n\n    orig = m.fused_topk\n\n    def wrapped(*a, **kw):\n        res = orig(*a, **kw)\n        try:\n            gating = kw.get(\"gating_output\")\n            if gating is None and len(a) > 1:\n                gating = a[1]\n            topk_ids = res[1] if isinstance(res, tuple) and len(res) >= 2 else None\n            n_local = gating.shape[-1] if topk_ids is not None and gating is not None else None\n            if topk_ids is not None and n_local:\n                _record(topk_ids, n_local)\n        except Exception:\n            pass  # never let the probe break routing\n        return res\n\n    wrapped._fn_orig = orig\n    m.fused_topk = wrapped\n    _installed = True\n    atexit.register(_flush)\n    print(...)\n```\n\nNote: FusedTopKRouter._compute_routing calls module-level fused_topk — if it references via module global, re-binding m.fused_topk works. If the router function calls fused_topk as a same-module global, re-binding the module attribute works. Good.\n\nAdd docstring note about the lazy-install rationale: adapted from upstream — \"by the first decode the MoE layers are long since built; import-time wrapping from an instrument load would risk circular imports\". Keep the two-stage gate note too.\n\n_flush: file name `expert_union_<pid>.tsv` — keep? For clarity of provenance use `fn_expert_union_<pid>.tsv`. Hmm, the upstream analysis tool host/bench/expert_union.py reads expert_union_*.tsv; flashnext's overseer \"spot check reads fn_expert_union.py\". Use `expert_union_<pid>.tsv` for drop-in compatibility? Analysis tool unknown; keep upstream name (TSV header same too). Print \"[fn_expert_union] wrote ...\".\n\nfn_offload_batch.py: rename only + notice. FRAC_ENV = \"FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC\", PROMOTE_FRAC_ENV = \"FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC\". Docstring mentions `$DS4_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC` → `$FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC`, `DS4_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC=0` → `FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC=0`. Keep measurements in comments (those are upstream measurements — with notice header, retainable; add \"(measured upstream)\" clarification? Comments are fine as-is).\n\nNext, tests/test_instruments.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Instrument overlay tests — compile, notices, and budget invariants.\n\nThe offline half for container/rootfs/fn_*.py: py_compile each module,\ncheck every adapted module carries its upstream notice, and check the\nfn_offload_batch budget arithmetic against the reference invariants\n(floor at the max group size; promote counted in blocks). fn_offload_batch\nis torch-free by design, so that module is imported and exercised directly.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport importlib.util\nimport pathlib\nimport py_compile\nimport tempfile\nimport unittest\n\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nROOTFS = ROOT / \"container\" / \"rootfs\"\nMODULES = (\"fn_synctrace.py\", \"fn_offload_batch.py\", \"fn_expert_union.py\")\n\n\ndef _load_offload_batch():\n    path = ROOTFS / \"fn_offload_batch.py\"\n    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"fn_offload_batch\", path)\n    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)\n    return mod\n\n\nclass InstrumentPackaging(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_modules_compile(self):\n        with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as td:\n            for name in MODULES:\n                path = ROOTFS / name\n                self.assertTrue(path.is_file(), f\"{path} missing\")\n                py_compile.compile(str(path), cfile=f\"{td}/{name}c\", doraise=True)\n\n    def test_notice_headers(self):\n        for name in MODULES:\n            text = (ROOTFS / name).read_text()\n            self.assertIn(\"Adapted from\", text, name)\n            self.assertIn(\"ds4-vllm\", text, name)\n            self.assertIn(\"Apache\", text, name)\n            upstream = \"ds4_\" + name[len(\"fn_\"):]\n            self.assertIn(upstream, text, f\"{name} must name {upstream}\")\n\n    def test_env_prefix_is_fn(self):\n        ... text checks: fn_expert_union.py contains FN_EXPERT_UNION, FN_EU_START, FN_EU_CALLS, FN_EU_OUT and no \"DS4_\" token; same for synctrace (FN_PROFILE); offload: FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC/FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC.\n```\n\nWait: careful — the notice header itself contains \"ds4\" (upstream file names). \"No DS4_ token\" check: notice mentions ds4_synctrace.py (no trailing underscore? \"ds4_\" + suffix — \"ds4_synctrace.py\" contains \"ds4_\"!). So assert \"DS4_\" not in text.upper()? That would match the \"DS4_SYNCTRACE.PY\" from the notice. Instead, check that specific env-var names aren't present: \"DS4_PROFILE\", \"DS4_EXPERT_UNION\", \"DS4_EU_\", \"DS4_OFFLOAD_\". These strings won't appear in the notice text (file names are ds4_synctrace.py etc.). Good.\n\nBudget arithmetic tests:\n\n```python\nclass OffloadBudgetInvariants(unittest.TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.mod = _load_offload_batch()\n\n    def test_store_floor_is_max_group_size(self):\n        m = self.mod\n        # tiny frac so the raw token budget lands under the coarsest block\n        budget = m.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16), frac=0.001)\n        self.assertEqual(budget, 128)  # floor bites exactly at max(sizes)\n\n    def test_store_budget_scales_with_frac(self):\n        m = self.mod\n        # frac 0.25 of 256 blocks = 64 blocks; rates 1/128 + 1/16 keys/token\n        self.assertEqual(m.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16), frac=0.25), 910)\n```\n\nLet me double-check: budget_blocks = max(1, int(256*0.25)) = 64. keys_per_token = 1/128 + 1/16 = 0.0078125 + 0.0625 = 0.0703125. 64 / 0.0703125 = 910.2222..., int → 910. Floor max(910, 128) = 910. ✓\n\nfrac=0.001: budget_blocks = max(1, int(0.256)) = 1; 1/0.0703125 = 14.222 → 14; max(14,128)=128 ✓ floor equals max group size.\n\n    def test_store_disabled_paths_return_none(self):\n        frac<=0 → None; num_blocks None/0 → None; empty sizes → None.\n\n    def test_promote_counted_in_blocks(self):\n        m = self.mod\n        b = m.resolve_promote_block_budget(258, frac=0.5)\n        self.assertEqual(b, 129)\n        # Block-counted: the result depends on num_blocks and frac only --\n        # group sizes do not enter the signature at all.\n        # Also invariant: never more than the tier, at least 1.\n        self.assertEqual(m.resolve_promote_block_budget(258, frac=2.0), 258)\n        self.assertEqual(m.resolve_promote_block_budget(1, frac=0.5), 1)\n        self.assertIsNone(m.resolve_promote_block_budget(258, frac=0))\n        self.assertIsNone(m.resolve_promote_block_budget(0))\n        self.assertIsNone(m.resolve_promote_block_budget(None))\n\n    def test_env_var_names(self):\n        m = self.mod\n        self.assertEqual(m.FRAC_ENV, \"FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC\")\n        self.assertEqual(m.PROMOTE_FRAC_ENV, \"FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC\")\n        self.assertEqual(m.DEFAULT_FRAC, 0.25)\n        self.assertEqual(m.DEFAULT_PROMOTE_FRAC, 0.5)\n\n    def test_env_overrides_frac(self):\n        with unittest.mock.patch.dict(os.environ, {m.FRAC_ENV: \"0.5\"}):\n            budget = m.resolve_store_batch_tokens(256, (128, 16))\n        expected: budget_blocks = int(256*0.5) = 128; 128/0.0703125 = 1820.44 → 1820. Assert 1820.\n```\n\nNote: frac parameter defaults to None → reads env. ✓\n\nAlso kill-switch default-off test for expert union + synctrace at text level:\n- fn_expert_union: `os.environ.get(\"FN_EXPERT_UNION\") == \"1\"` (default off).\n- Text check: contains 'FN_EXPERT_UNION\") == \"1\"'? Fragile to formatting. Instead, just check FN_EXPERT_UNION present and FN_EU_START/FN_EU_CALLS/FN_EU_OUT present.\n\nAnd \"default off\": fn_synctrace has no env var, install is only via explicit call — text check that it does not auto-install at import: assert \"install()\" appears but not called at module top-level... skip; too fragile. Stick to spec: py_compile, notices, budget invariants. Adding env pinning tests because they are the deliverable of the rename (\"rename to FN_ env prefix\").\n\nNow let me write everything. Also run acceptance commands before committing.\n\nOne more point on the fn_expert_union docstring's cost line: upstream claimed \"~4 extra small kernel launches per MoE call\". Our _record does: reshape/long, zeros, index-set, three scalar assignments — similar. Retain the adapted cost note, roughly: \"~4 small extra kernel launches per routing call; off unless FN_EXPERT_UNION=1.\"\n\nAlso delete upstream's \"Analyse the dump with host/bench/expert_union.py\"? flashnext doesn't have that file. Rephrase: \"Dump is a TSV: one row per recorded routing call.\" Fine.\n\nAlso note in the module docstring: why fused_topk: the flashnext workload routes through FusedTopKRouter → fused_topk (qsa-53896.md §4); upstream's make_routing_data target is gpt-oss triton_kernels path this workload doesn't hit.\n\nnum_local_experts from gating_output.shape[-1]: in FusedTopKRouter._compute_routing, fused_topk(hidden_states, router_logits, top_k, renormalize) — router_logits shape [num_tokens, num_experts].\n\nEdge case: some vLLM versions' fused_topk signature is (hidden_states, gating_output, topk, renormalize). The wrapper uses *a, **kw and only peeks at a[1]/kw[\"gating_output\"]; if different, n_local is None → skip record, never crash. Good.\n\nNow let me write the files. Line lengths modest.\n\nFull fn_synctrace.py:\n\n```python\n# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n# Adapted from ds4_synctrace.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d\n# (container/rootfs/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ds4_synctrace.py),\n# Apache License 2.0. Changes: DS4_ env surface renamed to FN_; the module is\n# driven from the flashnext FN_PROFILE window instead of ds4_tl_indexer.\n\"\"\"Attribute blocking device->host syncs to python call sites.\n\nThe upstream motivation (measured on ds4-vllm's 2026-08-07 profile):\nhipMemcpyWithStream at 42% of decode self-CPU and hipStreamSynchronize at\n16% -- together the dominant cost once kernel launches are down. But\n`hipMemcpyWithStream` in a profiler table is just a count; it does not say\nwhich line caused it.\n\n`with_stack=True` on the torch profiler answers that in principle and is\nunusable in practice here: it captures a python stack for every one of\n~3,200 ops per step, which pegged the worker at 94% CPU and stalled the\nengine on shm_broadcast.\n\nThis is the cheap alternative. It wraps only the handful of Tensor methods\nthat can force a blocking D2H, and records one `sys._getframe` per call --\n~25 events per step rather than ~3,200. Overhead is a dict increment on an\nalready-blocking operation, i.e. noise.\n\nLimitation worth knowing (retained from upstream): boolean-mask indexing\n(`x[mask]`) reaches nonzero() inside C++ `at::index`, not through the\npython method, so it is NOT counted here. Anything unattributed after this\nis a candidate for that.\n\nDriven from the flashnext FN_PROFILE window driver so it shares that\nwindow (no new env var of its own, hence no ray restart to propagate one).\nDefault off: nothing installs at import; install()/uninstall() bracket the\nwindow and are the only switches.\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nBody identical to upstream (names unchanged: install/uninstall/reset/report).\n\nfn_offload_batch.py: SPDX + notice + adapted docstring (rename the DS4 mention in the body? The docstring body mentions OffloadingConnectorScheduler etc. — keep). Internal references `$DS4_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC` → `$FN_OFFLOAD_STORE_BATCH_FRAC`, `DS4_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC=0` → `FN_OFFLOAD_PROMOTE_FRAC=0`.\n\nNote in the docstring \"This lives outside ``vllm`` so the budget arithmetic is unit-testable without importing torch...\" — keep.\n\nOK let me write the files. Then run acceptance commands, then commit.\n\nCommit message style — look at git log: \"container-recipe: Container recipe: pinned wheel set...\", \"receipts: weights staged...\", \"handoff: ...\". Tally probably has its own convention. Prefix \"instruments:\". Write like: \"instruments: Adapt the ds4-vllm engagement-proof instruments as fn_* overlay\".\n\nNow let me write the files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I have everything I need. The house conventions are clear (THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md already declares exactly these three adapted files with retained notices). Now writing the three adapted modules.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_84fcd37b76fd4c998373ad68","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/ed7013e7b4ab/instruments/container/rootfs/fn_synctrace.py","content":"# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n# Adapted from ds4_synctrace.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d\n# (container/rootfs/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ds4_synctrace.py),\n# Apache License 2.0. Changes: DS4_ surface renamed to FN_; the module is\n# driven from the flashnext FN_PROFILE window instead of ds4_tl_indexer.\n\"\"\"Attribute blocking device->host syncs to python call sites.\n\nThe upstream motivation (measured on ds4-vllm's 2026-08-07 profile):\nhipMemcpyWithStream at 42% of decode self-CPU and hipStreamSynchronize at\n16% -- together the dominant cost, once kernel launches are down. But\n`hipMemcpyWithStream` in a profiler table is just a count; it does not say\nwhich line caused it.\n\n`with_stack=True` on the torch profiler answers that in principle and is\nunusable in practice here: it captures a python stack for every one of\n~3,200 ops per step, which pegged the worker at 94% CPU and stalled the\nengine on shm_broadcast.\n\nThis is the cheap alternative. It wraps only the handful of Tensor methods\nthat can force a blocking D2H, and records one `sys._getframe` per call --\n~25 events per step rather than ~3,200. Overhead is a dict increment on an\nalready-blocking operation, i.e. noise.\n\nLimitation worth knowing (retained from upstream): boolean-mask indexing\n(`x[mask]`) reaches nonzero() inside C++ `at::index`, not through the\npython method, so it is NOT counted here. Anything unattributed after this\nis a candidate for that.\n\nDriven from the flashnext FN_PROFILE window driver so it shares that\nwindow (no new env var of its own, hence no ray restart to propagate one).\nDefault off: nothing installs at import; install()/uninstall() bracket the\nwindow and are the only switches.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport collections\nimport sys\n\nimport torch\n\n_counts = collections.Counter()\n_installed = False\n_orig = {}\n# Tensor methods that block on a device->host transfer.\n_TARGETS = (\"item\", \"tolist\", \"cpu\", \"numpy\", \"nonzero\", \"__float__\", \"__int__\")\n\n\ndef _wrap(name):\n    orig = getattr(torch.Tensor, name)\n\n    def wrapper(self, *a, **kw):\n        try:\n            f = sys._getframe(1)\n            _counts[f\"{f.f_code.co_filename.split('site-packages/')[-1]}:\"\n                    f\"{f.f_lineno} {f.f_code.co_name}() [{name}]\"] += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        return orig(self, *a, **kw)\n\n    _orig[name] = orig\n    setattr(torch.Tensor, name, wrapper)\n\n\ndef install():\n    global _installed\n    if _installed:\n        return\n    for n in _TARGETS:\n        try:\n            _wrap(n)\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    _installed = True\n\n\ndef uninstall():\n    global _installed\n    for n, o in _orig.items():\n        try:\n            setattr(torch.Tensor, n, o)\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    _orig.clear()\n    _installed = False\n\n\ndef reset():\n    _counts.clear()\n\n\ndef report(steps=1.0):\n    \"\"\"Formatted table, per-decode-step, most frequent first.\"\"\"\n    if not _counts:\n        return (\"### BLOCKING-SYNC ATTRIBUTION ###\\n  (no events -- either the \"\n                \"tracer never installed, or every sync is bool-mask indexing / \"\n                \"C++-internal and invisible to a python hook)\\n\\n\")\n    total = sum(_counts.values())\n    out = [f\"### BLOCKING-SYNC ATTRIBUTION ({total} events over {steps:.2f} \"\n           f\"decode steps = {total / max(steps, 1e-9):.1f}/step) ###\"]\n    for site, n in _counts.most_common(30):\n        out.append(f\"  {n / max(steps, 1e-9):7.1f}/step  ({n:5d})  {site}\")\n    out.append(\"\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\\n\"\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"qwen-token-plan","model":"qwen3.8-max","usage":{"input":1059,"output":8986,"cacheRead":38912,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":7885,"totalTokens":48957,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787988232741,"responseId":"chatcmpl-d68016cc-1bcc-4a42-8caf-22c4a50f3db4","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
{"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"call_84fcd37b76fd4c998373ad68","toolName":"write","args":{"path":"/home/tom/.local/state/tally/campaigns/flashnext/flashnext/ed7013e7b4ab/instruments/container/rootfs/fn_synctrace.py","content":"# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n# Adapted from ds4_synctrace.py in AlexKGwyn/ds4-vllm @ a8f620d\n# (container/rootfs/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/ds4_synctrace.py),\n# Apache License 2.0. Changes: DS4_ surface renamed to FN_; the module is\n# driven from the flashnext FN_PROFILE window instead of ds4_tl_indexer.\n\"\"\"Attribute blocking device->host syncs to python call sites.\n\nThe upstream motivation (measured on ds4-vllm's 2026-08-07 profile):\nhipMemcpyWithStream at 42% of decode self-CPU and hipStreamSynchronize at\n16% -- together the dominant cost, once kernel launches are down. But\n`hipMemcpyWithStream` in a profiler table is just a count; it does not say\nwhich line caused it.\n\n`with_stack=True` on the torch profiler answers that in principle and is\nunusable in practice here: it captures a python stack for every one of\n~3,200 ops per step, which pegged the worker at 94% CPU and stalled the\nengine on shm_broadcast.\n\nThis is the cheap alternative. It wraps only the handful of Tensor methods\nthat can force a blocking D2H, and records one `sys._getframe` per call --\n~25 events per step rather than ~3,200. Overhead is a dict increment on an\nalready-blocking operation, i.e. noise.\n\nLimitation worth knowing (retained from upstream): boolean-mask indexing\n(`x[mask]`) reaches nonzero() inside C++ `at::index`, not through the\npython method, so it is NOT counted here. Anything unattributed after this\nis a candidate for that.\n\nDriven from the flashnext FN_PROFILE window driver so it shares that\nwindow (no new env var of its own, hence no ray restart to propagate one).\nDefault off: nothing installs at import; install()/uninstall() bracket the\nwindow and are the only switches.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport collections\nimport sys\n\nimport torch\n\n_counts = collections.Counter()\n_installed = False\n_orig = {}\n# Tensor methods that block on a device->host transfer.\n_TARGETS = (\"item\", \"tolist\", \"cpu\", \"numpy\", \"nonzero\", \"__float__\", \"__int__\")\n\n\ndef _wrap(name):\n    orig = getattr(torch.Tensor, name)\n\n    def wrapper(self, *a, **kw):\n        try:\n            f = sys._getframe(1)\n            _counts[f\"{f.f_code.co_filename.split('site-packages/')[-1]}:\"\n                    f\"{f.f_lineno} {f.f_code.co_name}() [{name}]\"] += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        return orig(self, *a, **kw)\n\n    _orig[name] = orig\n    setattr(torch.Tensor, name, wrapper)\n\n\ndef install():\n    global _installed\n    if _installed:\n        return\n    for n in _TARGETS:\n        try:\n            _wrap(n)\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    _installed = True\n\n\ndef uninstall():\n    global _installed\n    for n, o in _orig.items():\n        try:\n            setattr(torch.Tensor, n, o)\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    _orig.clear()\n    _installed = False\n\n\ndef reset():\n    _counts.clear()\n\n\ndef report(steps=1.0):\n    \"\"\"Formatted table, per-decode-step, most frequent first.\"\"\"\n    if not _counts:\n        return (\"### BLOCKING-SYNC ATTRIBUTION ###\\n  (no events -- either the \"\n                \"tracer never installed, or every sync is bool-mask indexing / \"\n                \"C++-internal and invisible to a python hook)\\n\\n\")\n    total = sum(_counts.values())\n    out = [f\"### BLOCKING-SYNC ATTRIBUTION ({total} events over {steps:.2f} \"\n           f\"decode steps = {total / max(steps, 1e-9):.1f}/step) ###\"]\n    for site, n in _counts.most_common(30):\n        out.append(f\"  {n / max(steps, 1e-9):7.1f}/step  ({n:5d})  {site}\")\n    out.append(\"\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\\n\"\n"}}
